            <ArticleTitle>Treatment of One Case with Cryoglobulinaemia Secondary to Connective Tissue Disease with Small Doses of Rituximab.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>398-400</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7727/wimj.2014.384</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">wimj.2014.384</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Objective" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">This study aims to observe the clinical efficacy of low-dose rituximab in patients with cryoglobulinaemia secondary to connective tissue diseases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Rituximab (100 mg) was infused in patients once a week for four weeks, combined with prednisone (20 mg) once a day, and reduced regularly. Treatment effect was observed regularly.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Joint pain, fever, rash and fatigue symptoms in patients eased. The serology (globulin, erythrocyte sedimentation rate, C-reactive protein, lactate dehydrogenase and others) parameters returned to normal.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusion" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Low-dose rituximab therapy for cryoglobulinaemia secondary to connective tissue diseases was safe and effective, and can be used as a treatment option in this condition.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
